********************************************************************************
*                                                                              *
*              Readme file for the FIRE_ACE_SHIP_SSFR Data Set                 *
*                                                                              *
********************************************************************************

1.0 Introduction

    This file contains information about data provided by the Solar
Spectral Flux Radiometer (SSFR) during the Surface Heat Budget
of the Artic Ocean (SHEBA) field campaign which provided surface
measurements during the First ISCCP Regional Experiment (FIRE) 
Arctic Cloud Experiment (ACE) field campaign.  This data set is 
available at the Atmospheric Sciences Data Center as:

 	First ISCCP Regional Experiment Arctic Cloud Experiment 
	Ship Solar Spectral Radiometer (FIRE_ACE_SHIP_SSFR)

    Each data granule contains irradiance measurements for a single 
day. The data files range in size from 300 Kbytes to 3.0 Mbytes. 
The data files are written in netCDF (Common Data Format). NCSA's (National 
Center for Supercomputing Applications) HDF (Hierarchical Data Format) 
libraries with the netCDF extensions are required to build read software. 
These libraries can be obtained via anonymous ftp from ftp.ncsa.uiuc.edu or 
via the World Wide Web at http://hdf.ncsa.uiuc.edu.

    This readme file includes the following sections:

         Section 2.0 - Data Set Description
         Section 3.0 - Format and Packaging
         Section 4.0 - Science Parameters Information
         Section 5.0 - Description of Sample Read Software
         Section 6.0 - Implementing Sample Read Software
         Section 7.0 - Sample Output
         Section 8.0 - Additional Data Information 

    If you have any questions, please contact the Langley Atmospheric
Sciences Data Center (ASDC) Science, Users and Data Services Office at:

     Atmospheric Sciences Data Center
     Science, Users and Data Services Office
     Mail Stop 157D
     2 South Wright Street
     NASA Langley Research Center
     Hampton, Virginia 23681-2199
     U.S.A.

     E-mail:   larc@eos.nasa.gov
     Phone:    (757)864-8656
     FAX:      (757)864-8807
     URL:      http://eosweb.larc.nasa.gov

2.0 Data Set Description 

    The FIRE_ACE_SHIP_SSFR data set comes from the Surface Heat Budget of the 
Artic Ocean (SHEBA) project. An icebreaker was frozen into the perennial pack
ice and left to drift for a full year from October, 1997 to October, 1998. The
ship served as a floating scientific research station. During part of this time 
frame the FIRE ACE project was underway. FIRE ACE Aircraft flew over the SHEBA 
ice station site and collected radiation budget data. The FIRE_ACE_SHIP_SSFR 
data are from the SSFR instrument at that site giving ground measurements. 
This data can be compared with other FIRE ACE aircraft measurements also
available from the ASDC.

For information about the FIRE ACE and SHEBA missions see:

    http://eosweb.larc.nasa.gov/ACEDOCS/
    http://www.etl.noaa.gov/programs/1997/fireace/#instruments
and
    http://eosweb.larc.nasa.gov/ACEDOCS/data/sec4.1.1.html
    http://sheba.apl.washington.edu/

2.1 Instrumentation Description

    The Solar Spectral Flux Radiometer (SSFR) measured the 
incident irradiance over a spectral range of 400 to 1000 nm 
with a 5 nm spectral resolution and a sampling resolution of 1 nm.

2.2 Data Quality

    There are no known anomalies in these data.

2.3 Science Contact
       
    Questions pertaining to the data values contained within these files should be
directed to:

         Dr. Peter Pilewski
         NASA Ames Research Center
         Moffet Field, CA

         e-Mail: ppilewski@mail.arc.nasa.gov 

3.0 Format and Packaging 

    The following files are defined for this distribution:

    Filename                         Description
    ----------------------           -------------------- 
    readme_fire_ace_ssfr             This readme file. 
    read_fire_ace_ssfr.pro           IDL software to read the data files.

    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_05151998.cdf  The actual data files.
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_05161998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_05221998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_05231998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_05261998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_05271998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_05281998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_05291998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_05301998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_06071998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_06081998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_06091998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_06101998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_06121998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_06131998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_06151998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_06161998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_06181998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_06191998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_06221998.cdf
    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_06231998.cdf

The file naming convention used for the data files is:

    fire_ace_ship_ssfr_MMDDYYYY.cdf
       
    where 
            MM     -  Month
            DD     -  Day
            YYYY   -  Year

    For example:

	fire_ace_ship_ssfr_05161998.cdf
	
    This example is for a file that covers May 16th,1998.


4.0 Science Parameters Information

    The SSFR has the following fields defined:
 
    Field Name        Description
    --------------    -------------------- 
    Wavelength        400 to 1000 nm
    Time Data         GMT (hour.fractional hour)
    Irradiance        Watts per meter square per nm (Wm-2nm-1)
  
5.0 Description of Sample Read Software

    The sample read software (read_fire_ace_ssfr.pro) developed for the 
FIRE_ACE_SHIP_SSFR data set is written in the Interactive Data Language 
(IDL). The IDL program was developed and tested in IDL 5.3 on an SGI 
system. Information about IDL can be obtained at the Research 
Systems, Inc. web site at:

          http://www.rsinc.com/

6.0 Implementing Sample Read Software

    The user must have IDL version 5.3 and X-Windows running on his/her 
machine. Then the idl read software can be run from the command line:

    % idl
    IDL> read_fire_ace_ssfr

    A file dialog box opens and prompts the user to select a file to read.
The software then reads the file and writes out an ASCII file containing
the SSFR science data (see Sample Output below).

